---
/**
 * First run (U7: R8, R9).
 *
 * The recovery code section is the important one. Sanctum genuinely cannot
 * recover a lost passphrase, and a guide that soft-pedals that leaves people
 * to discover it at the worst possible moment.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Steps from '../../components/Steps.astro'
---

<Base
  title="First run - Sanctum guide"
  description="Create your vault, choose a passphrase, and save the recovery code that is shown only once."
>
  <section class="section wrap" style="border-top: none;">
    <h2>First run</h2>
    <p>Setting up takes about a minute. One step of it cannot be undone later.</p>

    <Steps steps={[
      'Run the installer and open Sanctum. There is no account to create and nothing to sign in to.',
      'Choose a passphrase. Sanctum requires a strength score of at least 3 and twelve characters &mdash; a few unrelated words beat a short scramble, and are easier to type twice a day.',
      'Wait a moment on first launch. Sanctum calibrates its key derivation to your machine, aiming for about three quarters of a second per unlock. A slower machine gets easier settings so unlocking stays usable; a faster one gets harder settings for free.',
      '<strong>Write down the recovery code.</strong> It is six groups of five characters, shown once, and it is the only other way into your vault.',
      'Confirm you have saved it. Sanctum will not show it again.',
    ]} />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>What the recovery code actually does</h2>
    <p>
      Your passphrase does not unlock records directly. It unwraps a key, and that key decrypts
      your data. The recovery code unwraps a second copy of the same key &mdash; which is why it
      can let you back in after a forgotten passphrase, and why it is exactly as sensitive as the
      passphrase itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep it somewhere separate from the machine running Sanctum. A password manager you already
      trust, a printed copy in a drawer, a safe. Not a note on the same desktop.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>If you lose both</h2>
    <p>
      The data is gone. There is no reset link, no support address that can help, and no backdoor
      &mdash; the key is derived from your passphrase and nothing else stores it. That is what makes
      the vault worth using, and it is a real risk you are accepting.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical answer is a backup: an encrypted <code>.sanctumbak</code> file with its own
      password, kept somewhere else. If the passphrase goes, the backup and its password are what
      is left.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
